Z={
1:"Commentators note mad-āśrayaḥ, “taking refuge in me”: the knowledge to come is for the one who depends on the Lord. Madhusūdana says the middle six chapters are the heart of the Gītā, joining the action of the first six with the knowledge of the last. Many devotees read chapters 7–12 most often.",
2:"Commentators note aśeṣataḥ, “completely”: Kṛṣṇa promises a full teaching. Madhusūdana says jñāna is knowing the Lord as the Self, and vijñāna is seeing him in all things. Some teachers compare the two to knowing the theory of music and hearing it played.",
3:"Commentators note siddhaye, “for perfection”: few even try. Madhusūdana says among those who try, few succeed, and among those, few know the Lord fully. The verse is meant to inspire effort, not discourage it.",
4:"Commentators note bhinnā, “divided”: nature is split into eight parts. Madhusūdana says these eight are the causes of all material things. Modern science also sees mind and brain as linked to matter; the Gītā places the knower beyond both.",
5:"Commentators note jagat dhāryate, “the world is held up”: life sustains the world. Madhusūdana says the higher nature is consciousness, which gives meaning to matter. Without consciousness, there would be no one to know the world.",
6:"Commentators note prabhava and pralaya, origin and dissolution: the Lord is both. Madhusūdana says the Lord is the cause of the world as gold is the cause of ornaments. Ornaments come and go; the gold remains.",
7:"Commentators note mattaḥ parataraṃ nānyat, “nothing higher than me”: the Lord is the highest reality. Madhusūdana says the thread is unseen but holds everything. Some teachers suggest a meditation: see the thread of awareness running through all your experiences of the day.",
8:"Commentators note that each item names the essence of a thing: water’s taste, the sun’s light, space’s sound. Madhusūdana says meditating on these essences leads to the Lord. Some teachers call this way of seeing “sacramental”: ordinary things become windows to the sacred.",
9:"Commentators note jīvanam, “life”: the Lord is the life in all beings. Madhusūdana says without life, a body is only matter. Many find in this verse reverence for all living things, plants and animals included.",
10:"Commentators note sanātanam, “eternal”: the Lord is the eternal seed. Madhusūdana says seeds of trees perish, but this seed never does. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (6.12) teaches through a banyan seed: break it open and you see nothing, yet from that nothing the great tree grows.",
11:"Commentators note bharatarṣabha, “bull of the Bharatas”: Kṛṣṇa speaks to Arjuna as a strong man. Madhusūdana says the strength of the strong is divine when it is free of selfish passion. A soldier protecting the innocent, a parent defending a child: such strength is sacred.",
12:"Commentators note bhāvāḥ, “states”: the guṇas are states of mind and matter. Madhusūdana says the Lord is their source but not their prisoner. The idea is developed fully in chapter 14.",
13:"Commentators note mohitam, “deluded”: the world is charmed by the guṇas. Madhusūdana says the charm hides the Lord. Some teachers compare it to watching a film so absorbing that we forget the screen.",
14:"Commentators note duratyayā, “hard to cross”: māyā cannot be crossed by effort alone. Madhusūdana says refuge in the Lord is the boat. The Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition calls this verse one of the foundations of the doctrine of surrender, prapatti.",
15:"Commentators note narādhamāḥ, “lowest of people”: those who know the good but do not do it. Madhusūdana says their knowledge is stolen by māyā. The verse warns that knowledge alone is not enough; it must change life.",
16:"Commentators note bhajante, “worship”: all four kinds of people turn to God. Madhusūdana says even the seeker of wealth is called virtuous because he turns to the right source. Many people come to faith first through need, and later stay through love.",
17:"Commentators note nitya-yuktaḥ, “ever united”: the wise one is always connected. Madhusūdana says the wise one’s love has no other object. The mutual love of the Lord and the wise devotee is the high point of this chapter.",
18:"Commentators note anuttamām gatim, “the unsurpassed goal”: the wise one rests in the Lord alone. Madhusūdana says the wise one is the Lord’s very self because nothing separates them. Even devotees who come for wealth are called noble.",
19:"Commentators note sudurlabhaḥ, “very rare”: the great soul who sees Vāsudeva everywhere is rare. Madhusūdana says many births of practice lead to this vision. Saints like Prahlāda, who saw the Lord in every pillar, are given as examples.",
20:"Commentators note prakṛtyā niyatāḥ svayā, “constrained by their own nature”: people worship according to their temperament. Madhusūdana says desire leads them to lesser deities. Śaṅkara himself is credited with organising worship of six deities, the ṣaṇmata, as different doors to the one Brahman.",
21:"Commentators note acalām, “unwavering”: the Lord strengthens every sincere faith. Madhusūdana says the Lord as inner controller makes faith steady. The verse supports respect for the faith of others, even when different from one’s own.",
22:"Commentators note mayaiva vihitān, “granted by me alone”: every blessing comes from the one source. Madhusūdana says the deities are channels, not sources. Many households begin worship with Gaṇeśa but end with a prayer to the one Lord behind all deities.",
23:"Commentators note alpa-medhasām, “of small understanding”: limited aims bring limited fruit. Madhusūdana says the same effort could win the highest. The verse is not a rebuke but an invitation to aim higher.",
24:"Commentators note avyayam anuttamam, “imperishable and supreme”: the Lord’s higher nature. Madhusūdana says the unwise see only the body of Kṛṣṇa. Many people in the epic saw Kṛṣṇa only as a cowherd or prince; only some saw more.",
25:"Commentators note mūḍhaḥ, “deluded”: the world does not recognise the Lord. Madhusūdana says the Lord hides himself from those who do not seek him. The veil lifts for the sincere, as it did for Arjuna in chapter 11.",
26:"Commentators note bhaviṣyāṇi, “future”: the Lord knows what is to come. Madhusūdana says no one knows the Lord except by his grace. The verse humbles the seeker and invites trust.",
27:"Commentators note sarge, “at birth”: beings are born into delusion. Madhusūdana says likes and dislikes are the first fruits of ignorance. Spiritual practice can be seen as slowly unlearning these automatic reactions.",
28:"Commentators note dṛḍha-vratāḥ, “firm in vows”: freed from opposites, devotees worship steadily. Madhusūdana says good deeds prepare the way. Many saints did years of service before deep devotion blossomed.",
29:"Commentators note jarā-maraṇa-mokṣāya, “for freedom from old age and death”: the oldest human longing. Madhusūdana says such seekers know Brahman fully. Many great spiritual journeys began with the shock of seeing ageing or death.",
30:"Commentators note yukta-cetasaḥ, “with minds united”: those whose minds are joined to the Lord remember him even at death. Madhusūdana says this verse introduces the theme of chapter 8. The connection shows how carefully the Gītā is built.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
